.TH BHIST-SPEEDUP 1 "October 2026" "Volclava 2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bhist-speedup \- faster bhist job history queries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bhist
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ job_ID " ...]"
.br
.B bhist-admin loader
.BR start | stop | restart | status | clean
.br
.B bhist-admin server
.BR start | stop | restart | status | view
.br
.B bhist-admin server perfmon
.IR seconds | \fBstop\fR
.br
.B bhist-speedup-setup
.RB [ \-\-remove ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Standard bhist builds job history by reading the
.B lsb.events
event files. Its queries slow down as the files grow larger and more
numerous. By default it reads only the current
.B lsb.events
and ignores earlier archives, and a query that spans many archives can use
so much memory that it runs out of memory.
.PP
bhist speed up keeps importing the event files into a local database in the
background. For a query, it first selects the events of the matching jobs
from the database, then has standard bhist format them. The output is the
same as standard bhist, common queries are much faster, and queries can
cover a much longer history.
.SH USING BHIST
Use
.B bhist
as before; nothing changes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bhist \-a \-u all
bhist \-l 12345
bhist \-C 2026/10/01/00:00,2026/10/20/23:59 \-u alice
.fi
.RE
.SS Accelerated queries
A query is accelerated when it uses only the following:
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-w
Long or wide format, not both.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-d ", " \-e ", " \-p ", " \-s ", " \-r
Filter by job state.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-q ", " \-m
Filter by user, queue, or host; each at most once.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-D ", " \-C
Filter by submit, dispatch, or completion time.
.TP
.I job_ID
One or more job IDs, including job arrays and array elements, for example
.BR 123 ,
.BR 123[4] ,
or
.BR 123[1-10] .
.PP
The options mean the same as in standard bhist; see
.BR bhist (1).
Any other query runs standard bhist directly, for example one with another
option
.RB ( \-n ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-J ,
and so on),
.BR \-h ,
or
.BR \-V .
.SS Differences from standard bhist
.TP
.B All kept history is searched by default.
Standard bhist reads only the current
.B lsb.events
unless
.B \-n
is given. An accelerated query returns what
.B "bhist \-n 0"
would, over all history kept in the database (the last 30 days by default,
as configured by the administrator).
.TP
.B New events appear with a delay.
A job change shows up a few seconds to a few tens of seconds later.
.SS When acceleration is unavailable
If the query fails in the bhist speed up service, bhist runs standard bhist
instead and prints:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bhist speed up: falling back to standard bhist
.fi
.RE
.PP
The results are still correct, only as slow as standard bhist. The usual
causes are that the service is not running or the database is still being
built. If this message keeps appearing, contact your cluster administrator.
.PP
If the query fails after part of the output has been printed, bhist does not
run standard bhist, to avoid printing the output twice. It prints the
following; run the query again:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bhist speed up: warning: results may be incomplete
.fi
.RE
.SS LSF clusters on CentOS
On CentOS releases earlier than 8, if
.B lsf.entitlement
or
.B profile.lsf
exists in
.B LSF_ENVDIR
(an LSF cluster), bhist always runs standard bhist.
.SH ADMINISTRATION
.SS Requirements
.IP \(bu 2
One host in the cluster serves as the service host. It may be a master
candidate or a compute host, but jobs should not be dispatched to it. It needs
a local disk (at least 200 GB is recommended) and must be reachable from all
cluster hosts.
.IP \(bu 2
bhist speed up was enabled at build or install time
.RB ( VOLC_BHIST_SPEEDUP=Y
in
.BR install.conf ).
.IP \(bu 2
SQLite on the service host is version 3.8.0 or later.
.IP \(bu 2
Management commands run on the service host as root or as the first cluster
administrator (the first user of
.B ClusterAdmins
in
.BR lsf.cluster. \fIcluster_name\fR).
.SS Configuration
Set two parameters in
.BR $LSF_ENVDIR/lsb.hist :
.PP
.RS
.nf
LSB_BHIST_ENDPOINT=hostA:3000
LSB_BHIST_DB_PATH=/var/lib/bhist-speedup/bhist-speedup.sqlite
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B LSB_BHIST_ENDPOINT
Service host name and port. The server listens on this port on all IPv4
addresses of the host; bhist connects to it by this host name.
.TP
.B LSB_BHIST_DB_PATH
Absolute path of the database file, on a local disk of the service host
(SSD recommended), not on NFS.
.PP
The loader finds the cluster's event directory by itself; set
.B LSB_BHIST_EVENT_DIR
only if the event files are elsewhere. For all parameters, see
.BR lsb.hist (5);
the defaults suit most hosts.
.SS Starting and stopping
The loader and server run on the service host named by
.BR LSB_BHIST_ENDPOINT .
Manage them in one of two ways.
.PP
.B Under systemd (recommended).
Run
.B bhist-speedup-setup
once as root. It creates the
.B volclava-bhist-loader
and
.B volclava-bhist-server
systemd services and enables them at boot; systemd restarts a service that
exits abnormally.
.PP
.RS
.nf
bhist-speedup-setup
systemctl start volclava-bhist-loader volclava-bhist-server
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B By hand.
.PP
.RS
.nf
bhist-admin loader start
bhist-admin server start
.fi
.RE
.PP
A service started with bhist-admin is not managed by systemd: it does not
start at boot and is not restarted if it exits. Do not mix the two ways; once
the systemd services are installed, start and stop with
.BR systemctl .
.PP
On its first start the loader imports all existing event files, which can
take a long time for a large cluster; until it finishes, bhist runs standard
bhist. Check progress with
.BR "bhist-admin loader status" ;
.B db_status=ready
means the database is ready.
.SS Rebuilding the database
When the loader log asks for a rebuild, or
.B bhist-admin loader status
shows
.BR db_status=rebuild_required ,
for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
state=stopped
log=/opt/volclava/log/bhist-speedup-loader.log.hostA
db=/var/lib/bhist-speedup/bhist-speedup.sqlite
eventsdir=/opt/volclava/work/logdir
db_status=rebuild_required
cluster=volclava
created=2026-10-22 10:37:41
event_rows_tracked=160340
.fi
.RE
.PP
rebuild the database:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bhist-admin loader stop
bhist-admin loader clean
bhist-admin loader start
.fi
.RE
.PP
With the systemd services installed, use
.B "systemctl stop volclava-bhist-loader"
and
.B "systemctl start volclava-bhist-loader"
for the first and last steps. bhist runs standard bhist during the rebuild;
the server keeps running. A rebuild restores only the history in event files
that still exist.
.SS Changing the configuration
.IP \(bu 2
Restart the loader and server after editing
.BR lsb.hist .
.IP \(bu 2
Stop the loader before changing
.BR LSB_BHIST_DB_PATH ;
otherwise it keeps writing to the old path.
.IP \(bu 2
Run
.B bhist-speedup-setup
again as root after the installation or platform directory changes.
.SS Removing the systemd services
.B "bhist-speedup-setup \-\-remove"
stops and removes both services; it keeps the database and logs. Uninstalling
Volclava does this automatically and reports the database directory, which
must be removed by hand.
.SH BHIST-ADMIN COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Start the loader or server.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the loader or server, however it was started.
.TP
.B restart
Stop, then start.
.TP
.B status
Show the state; see STATUS OUTPUT.
.TP
.B clean
Loader only. Delete the database so the next start rebuilds it from the
event files. Stop the loader first.
.TP
.B view
Server only. Show the latest performance sample.
.TP
.BI "perfmon " seconds
Server only. Start sampling performance every
.I seconds
seconds.
.TP
.B perfmon stop
Server only. Stop sampling.
.PP
With the systemd services installed, use
.B systemctl
for start, stop, and restart.
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR restart ,
and
.B clean
need root or the first cluster administrator;
.BR status ,
.BR view ,
and
.B perfmon
accept any cluster administrator.
.SH STATUS OUTPUT
.B bhist-admin loader status
prints, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
state=running
pid=2686406
log=/opt/volclava/log/bhist-speedup-loader.log.hostA
db=/var/lib/bhist-speedup/bhist-speedup.sqlite
eventsdir=/opt/volclava/work/logdir
db_status=ready
cluster=volclava
created=2026-10-21 11:00:54
event_rows_tracked=154118
systemd=managed
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B state
.B running
or
.BR stopped .
.TP
.BR pid ", " log
Process ID and log file.
.TP
.BR db ", " eventsdir
Database path and event directory.
.TP
.B db_status
.B ready
when usable;
.B missing
before the first import completes or before the first start;
.B rebuild_required
when the database must be rebuilt (see Rebuilding the database).
.TP
.BR cluster ", " created
Cluster the database belongs to, and when it was created.
.TP
.B event_rows_tracked
Number of events imported.
.TP
.B systemd
.B managed
when systemd runs the service,
.B not managed
when it was started by hand. Shown only when the systemd services are
installed.
.PP
.B bhist-admin server status
prints
.B state
.RB ( running ,
.B starting
when the process exists but does not yet accept connections, or
.BR stopped ),
.BR endpoint ,
.BR pid ,
.BR log ,
the queries running and queued
.RB ( running ", " queued ),
their limits
.RB ( max_running ", " max_scan_running ", " max_queued ;
see
.BR lsb.hist (5)),
and
.BR systemd .
.SH PERFORMANCE SAMPLING
.RS
.nf
bhist-admin server perfmon 60
bhist-admin server view
bhist-admin server perfmon stop
.fi
.RE
.PP
Example output of
.B view
after four 60-second periods:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Performance monitor start time: Wed Oct 21 16:56:18
End time of last sample period: Wed Oct 21 17:00:18
Sample period:                  60 Second(s)
REQUESTS
  Type                     Last          Max          Avg        Total
  job_id                      0            6          3.0           12
  time_range                  0            0          0.0            0
  history_scan                0            0          0.0            0
  TOTAL                       0            6          3.0           12
OUTPUT TO CLIENT
  Type                     Last          Max          Avg        Total
  TOTAL                     0 B     83.2 KiB     21.3 KiB     85.3 KiB
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B REQUESTS
counts queries by type:
.B job_id
names job IDs;
.B time_range
has no job ID but uses
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-D ,
or
.BR \-C ;
.B history_scan
has neither and searches all history.
.B OUTPUT TO CLIENT
is the data returned to users.
.B Last
is the latest period,
.B Max
the largest single period,
.B Avg
the average per period, and
.B Total
the sum since sampling started. In the example, 12 job ID queries were
handled, at most 6 in one period, 3 per period on average, and none in the
latest period.
.PP
Before the first period ends,
.B view
reports how many seconds remain. Samples are kept only in memory and are lost
when sampling stops or the server restarts.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
After each of the first four messages below, bhist also prints
.B "falling back to standard bhist"
and runs standard bhist; the results are still correct.
.TP
.B history database is not available yet
The database is not built yet, or the loader is not running. Check
.BR "bhist-admin loader status" ;
start the loader if it is stopped.
.TP
.B cannot connect to service
The server is not running, or the network is down. Check the server state,
.BR LSB_BHIST_ENDPOINT ,
and the firewall.
.TP
.B service error: ...
The server refused the request, for example because its queue is full.
Occasional messages can be ignored; if frequent, raise the concurrency or
queue limits.
.TP
.B history query failed
The server failed. Check the server log.
.TP
.B falling back to standard bhist
(alone) This host cannot run the accelerated client. Check that
.B bhist-fast
is installed.
.TP
.B results may be incomplete
The query failed after printing part of its output; bhist does not run
standard bhist. Run the query again, and check the server log if it recurs.
.TP
.B must run on the service host
A management command was run on another host. Log in to the host named by
.B LSB_BHIST_ENDPOINT
and run it there.
.TP
.B must be run as root or a cluster administrator
Run the command as root or a cluster administrator.
.TP
.B run this command as root or the service administrator
start and clean need root or the first cluster administrator.
.TP
.B the history database must be rebuilt
The loader stopped because the event files were truncated or replaced. Rebuild
the database (see Rebuilding the database).
.TP
.B already running
The service is already running, possibly started by systemd or someone else.
Nothing to do; use restart to restart it.
.TP
.B running ... log: ...
(the only line printed by a loader or server run in a terminal) Normal; the
rest of its output goes to the log file shown.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $LSF_BINDIR/bhist
The command users run, on any host. Accelerated queries go to the service;
others run standard bhist.
.TP
.B $LSF_BINDIR/bhist-fast
Sends queries to the service; run by bhist, not directly.
.TP
.B $LSF_BINDIR/bhist-standard
Standard bhist. bhist runs it when acceleration is not used, and the service
uses it to format output.
.TP
.B $LSF_SERVERDIR/bhist-speedup-loader
Imports the event files into the database and follows new events; runs on the
service host.
.TP
.B $LSF_SERVERDIR/bhist-speedup-server
Answers queries from bhist; runs on the service host.
.TP
.B $LSF_BINDIR/bhist-admin
Starts, stops, and inspects the loader and server.
.TP
.B $LSF_BINDIR/bhist-speedup-setup
Installs or removes the systemd services.
.TP
.B $LSF_ENVDIR/lsb.hist
Configuration, read on all hosts; see
.BR lsb.hist (5).
.TP
.B /etc/systemd/system/volclava-bhist-loader.service
.PD 0
.TP
.B /etc/systemd/system/volclava-bhist-server.service
.PD
The systemd services created by bhist-speedup-setup.
.TP
.BI $LSF_LOGDIR/bhist-speedup-loader.log. host
.PD 0
.TP
.BI $LSF_LOGDIR/bhist-speedup-server.log. host
.PD
Logs.
.TP
The file named by LSB_BHIST_DB_PATH
The database.
.SH SECURITY
Traffic between bhist and the server is not authenticated, and the server
does not verify who is querying: anyone who can reach the endpoint can read
all job history. Open the port only to the trusted cluster network.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsb.hist (5),
.BR bhist (1)
